== Damage Scaling ==
Damage scaling is a mechanic introduced in [[The Binding of Isaac: Afterbirth]] to provide a more challenging experience to players, enabling ''some'' enemies to retaliate against high damage attacks by deploying an "armor" of a sort to greatly reduce the amount of damage taken. Further details and an in-depth explanation may be found [[Damage Scaling|here]].

== Notes ==
* Using {{p|Chaos Card}} only kills one of its phases.
** {{dlc|nr}} If it is thrown upwards during its first phase, it can kill both phases if timed correctly.
* Using the {{p|Emergency Contact}} on Hush's second form appears to lower its damage resistance by 50% for approximately 5 seconds.
* Upon defeating Hush, Isaac gains 2 charges for his [[Active Collectible|active item]].
* {{p|A Card Against Humanity}} works in the Hush room. This can be done after the fight is completed to farm huge amounts of health and coins. Having {{i|Sack Head}} or {{t|Petrified Poop}} makes this strategy even more effective.
* {{dlc+|a+nr}} If Hush is killed for the first time and {{s|The Void}} is unlocked, Isaac can exit the room and come back in, and the entrance to The Void will appear without having to fight Hush again on a new run.{{dlc-}}
* Successfully using {{i|Eraser}} on Hush's first form prevents his second from spawning.

== Strategies ==
* Effects that cause long-lasting mass slowdown such as {{I|Stop Watch}} or the I'm Drowsy [[Pills|pill]] are very valuable, aiding the player's ability to dodge the complex tear patterns and deal with the massive spawns of enemies, which can happen in quick succession.
** The {{I|Broken Watch}} item can also cause a mass slow, but using it may not be a good idea since it also has a chance to speed up the entire room, making the fight extremely difficult.
** {{I|Night Light}} or {{I|Censer}} can apply tear slowdown on a smaller scale than the watch items, but their hard-to-control areas of effect may cause more confusion than benefit.
** {{Dlc|r}} The I'm Drowsy pill should '''not''' be used against Hush. The laser beams summoned in the final phase move at a speed based on Isaac's own base speed, but the slowdown from I'm Drowsy is not accounted for. Using it during the Hush fight will make it impossible to outrun the lasers.
* Any items that can block or deflect projectiles will be highly useful, due to the bullet-hell design of the fight. [[Orbital familiar]]s, {{i|Circle of Protection}}, {{i|The Soul}}, {{i|Trinity Shield}}, {{i|Dry Baby}}, {{i|Farting Baby}}, {{i|Host Hat}}, and {{i|Infamy}} may greatly help mitigate damage.
** Dry Baby's {{i|The Necronomicon}} effect will trigger very often due to the amount of projectiles, allowing to easily wipe out the large waves of enemies Hush can spawn.
** {{I|Psy Fly}} is especially helpful, as it both deflects a large number of projectiles, and only has a small cooldown after deflecting multiple.
** {{I|Saturnus}} may help with the large amount of bullets, but the tears in the ring at the start of the room may have fallen by the time Hush's shots reach you.
** {{I|Ocular Rift}} can trivialize most of Hush's attacks as projectiles are pulled towards the rifts and away from Isaac. However, rifts can also disrupt the projectile patterns, making dodging significantly more difficult.
* {{i|Lost Contact}} may be beneficial or detrimental depending on the player's tears stat; in combination with a high tears stat, many Lost Contact tears may still reach Hush even after blocking Hush's shots, but if the player has a low tears stat, they may find all their Lost Contact tears fired being destroyed during Hush's bullet hell attacks before they can reach their target.
** When the player has [[piercing tears]], however, Lost Contact can cause dodging most attacks to become trivial regardless of the player's tears stat, as the player's tears will destroy any enemy projectiles they come into contact with while remaining themselves intact.
* {{Dlc|nr}} {{i|Serpent's Kiss}}, {{i|Maw Of The Void}}, {{i|Athame}}, or {{i|The Virus}} (in combination with defensive items like {{p|Algiz}} or {{i|Book of Shadows}}) will make Hush's summoned enemies occasionally drop black hearts, allowing the player to farm large quantities of health during the enemy-spawning attacks.
** {{Dlc|r}} Serpent's Kiss only generates black hearts from contact damage. Maw Of The Void, Athame, and The Virus no longer generate black hearts at all.
* {{i|Broken Modem}} is not recommended, as it will stop Hush's tears and close some escape routes, making it harder to dodge in some cases.
** {{Dlc|r}} Tears affected by {{i|Broken Modem|n}} will be deleted rather than frozen in place, making the fight much easier.
* {{dlc|a+}} The combination of {{i|Mysterious Liquid}} and {{i|Trisagion|n}} can kill Hush extremely quickly by spawning massive amounts of creep under his large body.
* {{C|The Forgotten}}'s ability to swap characters into The Soul is very helpful in the Hush fight. Since nearly all of Hush's attacks are projectile-based, The Soul can hide behind the skeletal body and deal damage while staying totally safe.
* {{dlc|r}} Magnetizing Hush using {{I|Lodestone|n}} causes many of the slower bullets to not be able to go further than Hush's own hitbox, rendering the barrage ineffective.
* {{I|Cursed Eye}} can greatly benefit {{c|Keeper}}/{{c|Tainted Keeper}} if given the right items. They can fight Hush, and if they get damaged while charging, they teleport out of the fight and can go back to the shop area and bomb the {{M|Donation Machine}} to replenish health.
** {{T|Cursed Skull}} also works for this.
*** {{Dlc|r}}The {{M|Donation Machine}} can only be bombed once, after which it is destroyed for the rest of the run.
* Hush' {{e|Blue Gaper}}s spawning attack is useful for healing when playing as {{c|Tainted Magdalene}}.

== Trivia ==
* [https://twitter.com/edmundmcmillen/status/922696151651860480 According to Edmund on Twitter], Hush represents Isaac's imminent death slowly approaching towards him, which is the reason [https://twitter.com/edmundmcmillen/status/922697189842923521 why Hush and ??? are the same thing], since ??? represents Isaac suffocating in the chest, hence the blue color of their body.
** [https://twitter.com/edmundmcmillen/status/922698464198135808 According to Edmund on Twitter], Hush is a giant ??? pushing from the other side.
* In its first form (the replica of the ??? fight), "Ascension" plays, while in its second form, the name of the boss theme is "Morituros".
** Morituros in Latin is the accusative masculine plural for 'about to die', which roughly translates to "those who will die". This could also be interpreted as "death inevitable", since Isaac's unusual way of living was ought to have consequences at some point or another.
* Hush is one of only two bosses in the game with a [[Boss Room]] scaled in a 2x2 fashion, the other being {{E|Delirium}}.
* There is an unused file in the game named "boss_hush_skinless" which appears to be an unfinished sprite sheet for the unreleased third form of Hush. It resembles a red, fleshy head.
** Tyrone has stated that Hush's "skinless phase" was originally [https://twitter.com/tyronerodriguez/status/1103856012816113666 intended to be his second form, having concept art ideas based on it].
** {{dlc|a+}} It is possible to spawn it using console command. It moves but doesn't attack in any way.
* Hush shares his name with a level from Edmund's previous game, [[wikipedia:Super Meat Boy|Super Meat Boy]].
* As Hush loses health, the boss room grows gradually darker, and vein-like shadows begin to appear in the ceiling.
* When defeated, Hush screams and disappears in a flash of light. The ground where it once was becomes scorched in a shape resembling a distressed face.
* The 30-minute limit of reaching Hush's floor could be alluding to how long it took Isaac to suffocate in the chest.
* In the card game ''The Binding of Isaac: Four Souls'', Hush's card features an original translation of Job 4:16, ''"It stood still, but I could not discern its appearance; a form was before my eyes; at first there was a hush, and then I heard a voice."'' This might be due to Job 3, a monologue where Job curses the day of his birth.

